ubuntu-hello (1.1.5-1ppa1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Fixed the setup wizard getting stuck on "Testing your webcams, please
    wait...": scan_cameras_thread() had two unbounded blocking calls
    (udevadm subprocess, cv2.VideoCapture().read()) that could freeze the
    whole sequential device scan forever on a single misbehaving driver;
    both now have explicit timeouts (5s), with the VideoCapture open+read
    run on a watchdog thread so a stuck device is reported unopenable and
    skipped instead of blocking the scan indefinitely.
  * Fixed the live camera preview (shown while selecting a webcam) opening
    the raw /dev/v4l/by-path/... symlink instead of the resolved
    /dev/videoN device -- a regression from a later preview-thread
    rewrite that dropped the os.path.realpath() call present in v1.1.2
    and in the two sibling camera-opening call sites in the same file.
    Restored, matching v1.1.2's behavior.
  * Fixed an uncaught AttributeError in on_camera_selection_changed()
    (self.slide2_preview_image, which was never defined anywhere) that
    fired on every camera selection -- including the automatic first-row
    selection right after the webcam scan completes -- silently breaking
    the live preview immediately after "Testing your webcams" finishes.
    This is one of several distinct causes behind the wizard appearing
    stuck on that step; it does not exist in v1.1.2. The unit test suite
    never caught it because it runs against a mocked Gtk.Window that
    auto-vivifies any missing attribute instead of raising. Confirmed
    fixed by running the real installed binary against real webcam
    hardware: the traceback that fired on every run is gone.
  * Fixed the device list disappearing on the same webcam-testing step:
    update_camera_list_gui() builds the camera ScrolledWindow at runtime
    with no minimum content height, so GTK could allocate it ~0 size
    (logged as "Negative content width/height" warnings) and the device
    list silently vanished even though the scan succeeded and Next was
    enabled -- indistinguishable from a hang. v1.1.2 set
    set_min_content_height(100) on this widget; a later rewrite dropped
    it. Restored, matching v1.1.2.
  * Fixed a genuine crash (SIGSEGV) on the webcam-testing step, confirmed
    from a real terminal run: the device list's CellRendererText lost its
    ellipsize property in the same rewrite. Device names/paths can be
    arbitrarily long (full udev product strings, /dev/v4l/by-path/...
    paths), and the containing ScrolledWindow disables horizontal
    scrolling (PolicyType.NEVER); without ellipsizing, the resulting
    unbounded column-width request against a fixed-width parent drove
    GTK's layout code to compute a negative allocation
    ("gtk_widget_size_allocate(): attempt to allocate widget with width 0
    and height -15", followed immediately by "Negative content
    width/height" on the ScrolledWindow) and segfault. v1.1.2 set
    cell.set_property("ellipsize", Pango.EllipsizeMode.END); restored.
  * Fixed the actual root cause of the device list staying invisible
    (onboarding.glade): slidecontainer had the deprecated GtkBox
    resize-mode="immediate" property set. Isolated and reproduced in a
    minimal test: with this property set, any widget added at runtime to
    an already-realized/visible descendant box (exactly what
    update_camera_list_gui() does to devicelistbox once the scan
    finishes) permanently collapses to a 1x1 allocation, no matter how
    many further queue_resize() calls follow -- with the default resize
    mode the identical code sizes correctly. Removed the property.
    Confirmed by the user: the device list now renders normally after
    this change (a separate, expected issue remains where GStreamer
    itself fails to open some cameras -- reported per-device in the now-
    visible list rather than appearing as a stuck wizard).
  * Fixed the live camera preview always staying black even for a
    successfully-opened, compatible camera: update_preview_image_widget()
    referenced GdkPixbuf (aliased "pixbuf") to decode each captured
    frame, but that alias was only ever imported locally inside the
    sibling method open_camera_for_preview() -- never at module scope --
    so every call raised NameError: name 'pixbuf' is not defined,
    silently swallowed by a bare except. Diagnosed with temporary debug
    logging confirming frames were captured and encoded correctly
    (isOpened=True, frames read and PNG-encoded) but every widget update
    failed with this NameError. Fixed by importing GdkPixbuf as pixbuf
    at module level.
  * Fixed a VideoCapture handle leak in scan_cameras_thread()'s watchdog:
    when cap.read() failed after a successful open, or the watchdog timed
    out on a hanging read, the capture was never released. Left unfixed,
    this can leave the underlying device busy for later opens of the same
    real /dev/videoN node (this scan's own next iteration for a duplicate
    by-path alias, or the live preview). The watchdog now releases the
    capture itself whenever the read fails or the device is abandoned due
    to timeout.
  * Fixed two related concurrency bugs in that same watchdog, found in PR
    review: (1) the per-device open_result dict and abandoned Event were
    captured by the worker closure by reference, not bound per loop
    iteration, so a worker that finished after the scan had already moved
    on to a later device could read or write into that later device's
    state -- corrupting which camera's result was reported, or leaking a
    handle without being noticed; (2) the timeout handoff between the main
    thread (is_alive() + abandoned.set()) and the worker's own
    abandoned.is_set() check was not atomic, so a worker could observe
    "not abandoned" and publish its capture in the same window the main
    thread was declaring it abandoned, discarding that capture unreleased.
    Rewritten so each iteration owns a private state/lock pair (bound as
    default arguments, not captured by reference) and the lock makes the
    completion/timeout handoff atomic: whichever side reaches the pending
    state first owns the capture. The join timeout is now the module-level
    CAMERA_PROBE_TIMEOUT constant so tests can exercise the real watchdog
    path (a genuinely blocked read via threading.Event) instead of faking
    Thread.is_alive()/join(), which previously let a test pass without
    actually testing the timeout behavior. Added a regression test
    covering a device that times out and only later finishes, verifying
    it cannot corrupt a subsequent device's result.

 -- ventura8 <alexandrescu.sergiu@gmail.com>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 23:15:00 +0300

ubuntu-hello (1.1.4-1ppa1) resolute; urgency=medium

  * ARM64 release artifacts: .deb, Fedora RPM, openSUSE RPM, and AppImage now
    build and install-test for both amd64 and arm64 (arm64 via QEMU
    cross-build in release.yml, verified with the same live install ->
    upgrade -> remove -> reinstall E2E cycle as amd64). Arch, Snap, and
    Flatpak stay amd64-only (no official arm64 archlinux image; systemd/snapd
    under QEMU is unreliable; flatpak-builder's bwrap sandbox needs user
    namespaces, which QEMU user-mode emulation does not support).
  * Fixed AppImage arm64 "Exec format error": type-2 AppImages encode an
    "AI\x02" signature into ELF e_ident bytes that the standard
    qemu-user-static binfmt_misc registration rejects. appimagetool
    extraction and our own built AppImage's E2E extraction now compute the
    squashfs offset from the ELF header and use unsquashfs on the primary
    path, falling back to direct execution of the wrapped AppImage only if
    unsquashfs or offset detection is unavailable.
  * Packaging script hardening: RPM/AppImage artifact selection now matches
    this cell's own architecture instead of a bare wildcard; debian/rules
    cleans only the current build's own meson output dir.

